            <TABLE cellSpacing=3D0 cellPadding=3D0 width=3D"100%" =
border=3D0>
              <TBODY>
              <TR vAlign=3Dtop>
                <TD width=3D"69%"><FONT face=3DArial><A=20
                  name=3Dwhat></A></FONT><B><FONT face=3DArial>What is=20
                  Irrigation?</FONT></B><BR><BR><FONT face=3DArial=20
                  size=3D2>Irrigation is the artificial application of =
water to=20
                  land for the purpose of agricultural production. =
Effective=20
                  irrigation will influence the entire growth process =
from=20
                  seedbed preparation, germination, root growth, =
nutrient=20
                  utilisation, plant growth and regrowth, yield and =
quality.=20
                  </FONT><BR><BR><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>The key to =
maximising=20
                  irrigation efforts is uniformity. The producer has a =
lot of=20
                  control over how much water to supply and when to =
apply it but=20
                  the irrigation system determines uniformity. Deciding =
which=20
                  irrigation systems is best for your operation requires =
a=20
                  knowledge of equipment, system design, plant species, =
growth=20
                  stage, root structure, soil composition, and land =
formation.=20
                  Irrigation systems should encourage plant growth while =

                  minimising salt imbalances, leaf burns, soil erosion, =
and=20
                  water loss. Losses of water will occur due to =
evaporation,=20
                  wind drift, run-off and water (and nutrients) sinking =
deep=20
                  below the root zone.</FONT><BR><BR><FONT face=3DArial=20
                  size=3D2>Proper irrigation management takes careful=20
                  consideration and vigilant =
observation.<BR></FONT><BR><A=20
                  =
href=3D"http://www.dpi.vic.gov.au/dpi/nrenfa.nsf/LinkView/F0B43DC7D455B76=
94A256B4800125F2211AF46B80DF604E0CA256C4B00033C54#top"><FONT=20
                  face=3DArial size=3D2>Page Top</FONT></A></TD>
                <TD width=3D"31%"><BR><BR>
                  <DIV align=3Dright><IMG height=3D172=20
                  alt=3D"Photo: A water wheel used in irrigation =
systems" hspace=3D5=20
                  =
src=3D"http://www.dpi.vic.gov.au/CA25677D007DC87D/LUbyDesc/Water+wheel-2/=
$File/wheel.jpg"=20
                  width=3D250 =
vspace=3D2></DIV></TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE><BR><FONT=20
            face=3DArial size=3D2><A name=3Dvalue></A></FONT><B><FONT =
face=3DArial>The=20
            Value of Irrigation</FONT></B><BR><BR><FONT face=3DArial=20
            size=3D2>Irrigation allows primary producers,</FONT>=20
            <UL>
              <LI type=3Ddisc><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>to grow more =
pastures and=20
              crops<BR><BR></FONT>
              <LI type=3Ddisc><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>to have more =
flexibility in=20
              their systems/operations as the ability to access water at =
times=20
              when it would otherwise be hard to achieve good plant =
growth (due=20
              to a deficit in soil moisture) is imperative. Producers =
can then=20
              achieve higher yields and meet market/seasonal demands =
especially=20
              if rainfall events do no occur.<BR><BR></FONT>
              <LI type=3Ddisc><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>to produce =
higher quality=20
              crops/pastures as water stress can dramatically impact on =
the=20
              quality of farm produce<BR><BR></FONT>
              <LI type=3Ddisc><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>to lengthen =
the growing=20
              season (or in starting the season at an earlier=20
              time)<BR><BR></FONT>
              <LI type=3Ddisc><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>to have =
'insurance' against=20
              seasonal variability and drought.<BR><BR></FONT>
              <LI type=3Ddisc><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>to stock more =
animals per=20
              hectare and practice tighter grazing management due to the =

              reliability of pasture supply throughout the =
season<BR><BR></FONT>
              <LI type=3Ddisc><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>to maximise =
benefits of=20
              fertiliser applications. Fertilisers need to be =91watered =
into=92 the=20
              ground in order to best facilitate plant growth. =
<BR><BR></FONT>
              <LI type=3Ddisc><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>to use areas =
that would=20
              otherwise be =91less productive=92. Irrigation can allow =
farmers to=20
              open up areas of their farms where it would otherwise be =
=91too dry=92=20
              to grow pasture/crops. This also gives them the capability =
to=20
              carry more stock or to conserve more feed.<BR><BR></FONT>
              <LI type=3Ddisc><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>to take =
advantage of market=20
              incentives for unseasonal production <BR><BR></FONT>
              <LI type=3Ddisc><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>to have less =
reliance on=20
              supplementary feeding (grain, hay) in grazing operations =
due to=20
              the more consistent supply &amp; quality of pastures grown =
under=20
              irrigation<BR><BR></FONT>
              <LI type=3Ddisc><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>to improve the =
capital value=20
              of their property. Since irrigated land can potentially =
support=20
              higher crops, pasture and animal production, it is =
considered more=20
              valuable. The value of the property is also related to the =
water=20
              licensing agreements or =91water right=92.<BR><BR></FONT>
              <LI type=3Ddisc><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>to cost =
save/obtain greater=20
              returns. The cost benefits from the more effective use of=20
              fertilisers and greater financial benefits as a result of =
more=20
              effective agricultural productivity (both quality and =
quantity)=20
              and for =91out of season=92 production are =
likely.</FONT></LI></UL><FONT=20
            face=3DArial size=3D2><A name=3Dtypes></A></FONT><B><FONT =
face=3DArial>Types=20
            of Irrigation Systems</FONT></B><FONT face=3DArial> =
</FONT><FONT=20
            face=3DArial size=3D2></FONT><BR><FONT face=3DArial =
size=3D2><BR></FONT>
            <TABLE width=3D"100%" border=3D1>
              <TBODY>
              <TR vAlign=3Dtop>
                <TD width=3D"24%"><B><FONT face=3DArial =
size=3D2>Irrigation=20
                  system</FONT></B></TD>
                <TD width=3D"76%"><B><FONT face=3DArial=20
                  size=3D2>Description</FONT></B></TD></TR>
              <TR vAlign=3Dtop>
                <TD width=3D"24%"><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>Furrow=20
                systems</FONT></TD>
                <TD width=3D"76%"><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>This =
system comprises=20
                  a series of small, shallow channels used to guide =
water down a=20
                  slope across a paddock. Furrows are generally =
straight, but=20
                  may also be curved to follow the contour of the land,=20
                  especially on steeply sloping land. Row crops are =
typically=20
                  grown on the ridge or bed between the furrows, spaced =
from 1=20
                  meter apart. </FONT></TD></TR>
              <TR vAlign=3Dtop>
                <TD width=3D"24%"><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>Flood or =
border check=20
                  systems</FONT></TD>
                <TD width=3D"76%"><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>These =
systems divide=20
                  the paddock into bays separated by parallel =
ridges/border=20
                  checks. Water flows down the paddock's slope as a =
sheet guided=20
                  by ridges. On steeply sloping lands, ridges are more =
closely=20
                  spaced and may be curved to follow the contour of the =
land.=20
                  Border systems are suited to orchards and vineyards, =
and for=20
                  pastures and grain crops.</FONT></TD></TR>
              <TR vAlign=3Dtop>
                <TD width=3D"24%"><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>Level =
basin=20
                  systems</FONT></TD>
                <TD width=3D"76%"><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>These =
systems differ=20
                  from traditional border check or flood systems in that =
slope=20
                  of the land is level and area's ends are closed. Water =
is=20
                  applied at high volumes to achieve an even, rapid =
ponding of=20
                  the desired application depth within basins. =
</FONT></TD></TR>
              <TR vAlign=3Dtop>
                <TD width=3D"24%"><FONT face=3DArial =
size=3D2>Center-pivot sprinkler=20
                  systems</FONT></TD>
                <TD width=3D"76%"><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>A =
center-pivot=20
                  sprinkler is a self-propelled system in which a single =

                  pipeline supported by a row of mobile towers is =
suspended 2 to=20
                  4 meters above ground. Water is pumped into the =
central pipe=20
                  and as the towers rotate slowly around the pivot =
point, a=20
                  large circular area is irrigated. Sprinkler nozzles =
mounted on=20
                  or suspended from the pipeline distribute water under =
pressure=20
                  as the pipeline rotates. The nozzles are graduated =
small to=20
                  large so that the faster moving outer circle receives =
the same=20
                  amount of water as the slower moving inside. =
</FONT></TD></TR>
              <TR vAlign=3Dtop>
                <TD width=3D"24%"><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>Hand move =
sprinkler=20
                  systems</FONT></TD>
                <TD width=3D"76%"><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>Hand move =
sprinkler=20
                  systems are a series of lightweight pipeline sections =
that are=20
                  moved manually for successive irrigations. Lateral =
pipelines=20
                  are connected to a mainline, which may be portable or =
buried.=20
                  Handmove systems are often used for small, irregular =
areas.=20
                  Handmove systems are not suited to tall-growing field =
crops=20
                  due to difficulty in repositioning laterals. Labor=20
                  requirements are higher than for all other=20
                sprinklers.</FONT></TD></TR>
              <TR vAlign=3Dtop>
                <TD width=3D"24%"><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>Solid set =
/ fixed=20
                  sprinkler systems</FONT></TD>
                <TD width=3D"76%"><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>Solid set =
/fixed refer=20
                  to a stationary sprinkler system. Water-supply =
pipelines are=20
                  generally fixed (usually below the soil surface) and =
sprinkler=20
                  nozzles are elevated above the surface. Solid-set =
systems are=20
                  commonly used in orchards and vineyards for frost =
protection=20
                  and crop cooling. Solid-set systems are also widely =
used on=20
                  turf and in landscaping.</FONT></TD></TR>
              <TR vAlign=3Dtop>
                <TD width=3D"24%"><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>Travelling =
gun=20
                  sprinkler systems</FONT></TD>
                <TD width=3D"76%"><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>Travelling =
gun systems=20
                  use a large sprinkler mounted on a wheel or trailer, =
fed by a=20
                  flexible rubber hose. The sprinkler is self-propelled =
while=20
                  applying water, travelling in a lane guided by a =
cable. The=20
                  system requires high operating pressures, with 100 psi =
not=20
                  uncommon. </FONT></TD></TR>
              <TR vAlign=3Dtop>
                <TD width=3D"24%"><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>Side-roll =
wheel-move=20
                  systems</FONT></TD>
                <TD width=3D"76%"><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>Side-roll =
wheel-move=20
                  systems have large-diameter wheels mounted on a =
pipeline,=20
                  enabling the line to be rolled as a unit to successive =

                  positions across the field. Crop type is an important=20
                  consideration for this system since the pipeline is =
roughly 1=20
                  meter above the ground. </FONT></TD></TR>
              <TR vAlign=3Dtop>
                <TD width=3D"24%"><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>Linear or =
lateral-move=20
                  systems</FONT></TD>
                <TD width=3D"76%"><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>Linear or =
lateral-move=20
                  systems are similar to center-pivot systems, except =
that the=20
                  lateral line and towers move in a continuous straight =
path=20
                  across a rectangular field. Water may be supplied by a =

                  flexible hose or pressurised from a concrete-lined =
ditch along=20
                  the field's edge.</FONT></TD></TR>
              <TR vAlign=3Dtop>
                <TD width=3D"24%"><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>Low-flow =
irrigation=20
                  systems (including drip and trickle)</FONT></TD>
                <TD width=3D"76%"><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>Low-flow =
irrigation=20
                  systems (including drip and trickle) use =
small-diameter tubes=20
                  placed above or below the soil's surface. Frequent, =
slow=20
                  applications of water are applied to the soil through =
small=20
                  holes or emitters. The emitters are supplied by a =
network of=20
                  main, submain, and lateral lines. Water is dispensed =
directly=20
                  to the root zone, avoiding runoff or deep percolation =
and=20
                  minimising evaporation. These systems are generally =
used in=20
                  orchards, vineyards, or high-valued vegetable crops.=20
              </FONT></TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE><BR><A=20
            =
href=3D"http://www.dpi.vic.gov.au/dpi/nrenfa.nsf/LinkView/F0B43DC7D455B76=
94A256B4800125F2211AF46B80DF604E0CA256C4B00033C54#top"><FONT=20
            face=3DArial size=3D2>Page Top</FONT></A><BR><BR><FONT =
face=3DArial=20
            size=3D2><A name=3Dchoosing></A></FONT><B><FONT =
face=3DArial>Choosing an=20
            Irrigation System</FONT></B><FONT face=3DArial> =
</FONT><BR><BR><FONT=20
            face=3DArial size=3D2>There is a huge diversity in the types =
of=20
            irrigation technologies/systems used, which is attributable=20
            to,</FONT>=20
            <UL type=3Ddisc>
              <LI><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>Variations in soil =
types</FONT>=20
              <LI><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>Varying topography of the =
land</FONT>=20
              <LI><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>Availability of power =
sources</FONT>=20
              <LI><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>Availability of =
water</FONT>=20
              <LI><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>Sources of water</FONT>=20
              <LI><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>The period of time when =
the system was=20
              installed</FONT>=20
              <LI><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>The size of the area being =

              irrigated</FONT>=20
              <LI><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>On farm water storage =
capacity</FONT>=20
              <LI><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>Availability of =
labour/financial=20
              resources</FONT></LI></UL><A=20
            =
href=3D"http://www.dpi.vic.gov.au/dpi/nrenfa.nsf/LinkView/F0B43DC7D455B76=
94A256B4800125F2211AF46B80DF604E0CA256C4B00033C54#top"><FONT=20
            face=3DArial size=3D2>Page Top</FONT></A><FONT face=3DArial=20
            size=3D2><BR></FONT><BR><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2><A=20
            name=3Dsource></A></FONT><B><FONT face=3DArial>Source of =
Irrigation=20
            Water</FONT></B><B><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2> =
</FONT></B><BR><BR><FONT=20
            face=3DArial size=3D2>The vast majority of irrigation water =
use is=20
            pumped directly from a water source (river, creek, channel, =
drag=20
            line, hole, dam or bore).<BR></FONT><BR><FONT face=3DArial =
size=3D2><A=20
            name=3Dirrigation></A></FONT><B><FONT =
face=3DArial>Irrigation Scheduling=20
            </FONT></B><B><FONT face=3DArial =
size=3D2><BR></FONT></B><BR><FONT=20
            face=3DArial size=3D2>Irrigation scheduling is the process =
by which an=20
            irrigator determines the timing and quantity of water to be =
applied=20
            to the crop/pasture. The challenge is to estimate crop water =

            requirements for different growth stages and climatic =
conditions.=20
            </FONT>
            <P><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>To avoid over or under =
watering, it is=20
            important to know how much water is available to the plant, =
and how=20
            efficiently the plant can use it. The methods available to =
measure=20
            this include: (i) plant observation, (ii) feel and =
appearance of the=20
            soil, (iii) using soil moisture monitoring devices; or (iv)=20
            estimating available water from weather data. <BR></FONT>
            <P>
            <TABLE width=3D"100%" border=3D1>
              <TBODY>
              <TR vAlign=3Dtop>
                <TD width=3D"14%"><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>Plant=20
                  observation:</FONT></TD>
                <TD width=3D"86%"><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>Visible =
changes in=20
                  plant characteristics, such as leaf colour, curling of =
the=20
                  leaves and ultimately wilting can be useful guides to =
indicate=20
                  plant moisture stress, and hence the need for =
irrigation.=20
                  Productivity may be lowered, particularly if moisture=20
                  depletion is allowed to the point where wilting =
occurs. The=20
                  moisture status of plants can also be measured using =
sap flow=20
                  sensors (used mainly for research), infra-red guns =
(used in=20
                  the cotton industry) and pressure bombs (which measure =
leaf=20
                  water potential). </FONT></TD></TR>
              <TR vAlign=3Dtop>
                <TD width=3D"14%"><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>Feel and =
appearance of=20
                  the soil:</FONT></TD>
                <TD width=3D"86%"><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>Visual =
observation and=20
                  feel of the soil is used to monitor moisture levels of =

                  paddocks and hence their ability to sustain plant =
growth. A=20
                  soil sample can be obtained by using a soil probe, =
auger or=20
                  spade. By squeezing soil into a ball, observing the =
appearance=20
                  of the ball and creating a ribbon of soil between the =
thumb=20
                  and forefinger, soil moisture can be estimated (see =
</FONT><A=20
                  =
href=3D"http://www.dpi.vic.gov.au/dpi/nreninf.nsf/LinkView/6DC2035BC71797=
14CA256C1700047863FC1ACD73B37596414A256DEA0027E49F"><FONT=20
                  face=3DArial size=3D2>Agriculture Note AG0787: =
Irrigation=20
                  scheduling for vegetable crops</FONT></A><FONT =
face=3DArial=20
                  size=3D2>). For example,<BR><BR></FONT><B><FONT =
face=3DArial=20
                  size=3D2>At 75 % field capacity</FONT></B><FONT =
face=3DArial=20
                  size=3D2>, (i) Sands and sandy loams - are slightly =
coherent,=20
                  will form a weak ball under pressure but breaks =
easily, (ii)=20
                  Loams, clay loams and clays- are coherent, soil has a =
slick=20
                  feeling and ribbons easily, and will not roll into =
long thin=20
                  rods 2.5 diameter, and (iii) Comment - there is =
adequate water=20
                  and air for good plant growth<BR></FONT><BR><B><FONT=20
                  face=3DArial size=3D2>At 0-25 % field =
capacity</FONT></B><FONT=20
                  face=3DArial size=3D2> (or wilting point), (i) Sands =
and sandy=20
                  loams - are dry, loose, flows through fingers, (ii) =
Loams,=20
                  clay loams and clays are crumbly and powdery, small =
lumps=20
                  break into powder, and will not ball under pressure, =
and (iii)=20
                  Comment - plants desperately need watering and will =
die=20
                  soon.</FONT></TD></TR>
              <TR vAlign=3Dtop>
                <TD width=3D"14%"><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>Weather =
based=20
                  data:</FONT></TD>
                <TD width=3D"86%"><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>There are =
two weather=20
                  - based scheduling systems used to measure the amount =
of water=20
                  lost from a crop. These are: (i) Evaporation from an =
open=20
                  water surface -gives some indication of crop water use =
(the=20
                  latter is generally lower), or (ii) Historical climate =
data=20
                  such as relative humidity, temperature, wind speed and =

                  sunshine hours.</FONT></TD></TR>
              <TR vAlign=3Dtop>
                <TD width=3D"14%"><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>Soil =
moisture=20
                  monitoring:</FONT></TD>
                <TD width=3D"86%"><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>Soil =
moisture can be=20
                  measured as a suction or volume of water. This idea is =

                  applicable to how much force a plant can exert on the =
soil to=20
                  extract the amount of water it needs for growth. Soil =
moisture=20
                  suction can be used as a measure of plant stress and =
for that=20
                  reason it is a handy tool for growers to use in =
scheduling=20
                  their irrigations. Soil moisture monitoring devices =
include=20
                  tensiometers and resistance (measure soil moisture =
suction) or=20
                  neutron probe, EnviroScan=AE, Gopher=AE, Time Domain =
Reflectometry=20
                  (TDR), DRW Microlink=AE and Aquaflex (measure soil =
moisture=20
                  content). </FONT></TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE><BR><A=20
            =
